{
  "dom_id": "step-q212209",
  "name": "Question 11",
  "problem": "Which of the following relations are irreflexive? \n\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $x-y\\ge -1,$ defined on $Q$\n\n- $x\\,S\\,y$ if and only if $xy\\le 0,$ defined on $Z$\n\n- $x\\,T\\,y$ if and only if $x>y,$ defined on $R$",
  "worked_solution": "A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is \n\n- *reflexive* if $(x,x)\\in R$ for all $x\\in A,$\n\n- *irreflexive* if $(x,x)\\notin R$ for all $x\\in A.$\n\nIf some pairs of the form $(x,x)$ belong to a relation and others do not, then the relation is neither reflexive nor irreflexive.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our relations.\n\n- Relation $R$ is reflexive. Indeed, $(x,x)\\in R$ for all $x\\in Q.$ Notice that \n\n$$\nx-x=0\\ge -1\n$$\n\nfor any $x\\in Q.$\n\n- Relation $S$ is neither reflexive nor irreflexive. Notice that \n\n- $(1,1)\\notin S$ since $1\\cdot 1=1≰0,$ while\n\n- $(0,0)\\in S$ since $0\\cdot 0=0\\le 0.$\n\n- Relation $T$ is irreflexive. Indeed, $(x,x)\\notin T$ for all $x\\in R.$ Notice that \n\n$$\nx≯x\n$$\n\nfor any $x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$T$ only.\"",
